Notebook[{
Cell["Five-point MRK with a central-soft composite limit", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"f9d2816e-e072-4299-89a2-be4e5ceb71b2"],

Cell["\<\
Exact-kinematics asymptotic-order-alignment audit. Evaluate cells in order in \
a fresh Mathematica 15.0.1 kernel. The compact outputs below are saved so \
that the physics conclusion can be read without rerunning the scans.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"97fdda2d-acba-4cba-becb-8adcdbaa8f8b"],

Cell["1. Result at a glance", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"2388f6aa-5295-4e41-bc14-76caaf980def"],

Cell["\<\
Generic five-point MRK and the composite limit p4_perp -> 0 are not the same \
expansion. In the scan recorded here, all inequivalent one-loop pentagon \
attachment labelings have no hidden region. The two-loop six-propagator seed \
has no certified hidden region in generic MRK, but has one in the composite \
limit for six of the twelve inequivalent graph attachment labelings. Every \
accepted labeling attaches the central gluon p4 to internal vertex 3 or 5, \
the two four-valent vertices of the seed graph, so this is a necessary \
condition within the scan but not sufficient: {1,3,2,5,4} also has p4 at \
vertex 5 and is negative. The direct endpoint-reflected partner {1,2,4,5,3} \
is positive. These graph labelings are not alternative rapidity orderings: \
every row uses y3 >> y4 >> y5.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ff966ea1-e82f-471c-89e2-dcb44e099cab"],

