Cell["2. Five-point MRK kinematics", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"29fe21ba-8c81-4d44-9167-4885b03c6124"],

Cell["\<\
We use the all-outgoing convention -p1,-p2 -> p3,p4,p5. The physical region \
has s12,s34,s45>0 and s23,s15<0. Ordinary MRK is p3+ >> p4+ >> p5+ and p3- << \
p4- << p5-, with transverse momenta of the same parametric size. Section 2 of \
NNLL exploration of the high-energy limit of QCD 2->3 scattering amplitudes \
writes s12=s/x^2, s34=s2/x, s45=s1/x, s23=t2 and s15=t1.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b57ac311-34e1-4374-9275-9d56fcf5f0fc"],

Cell["\<\
At leading MRK order, p3_perp=-z p4_perp and p5_perp=-(1-z)p4_perp, while \
|p4_perp|^2=s1 s2/s. Therefore p4_perp -> 0 at fixed momentum transfers is \
correlated: z and zbar diverge, p3_perp=-p5_perp at the limiting surface, and \
t1=t2 there.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b5366c51-60ad-4e51-8d22-f3c0f67c13f8"],

Cell["Exact common chart", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"722ace6e-e537-4694-a690-4c4c680bf669"],

Cell["\<\
Use w=-p3_perp/p4_perp and define zeta=-1/w=p4_perp/p3_perp, together with \
its barred partner. Let Q4^2=|p4_perp|^2, X34=p3+/p4+ and X45=p4+/p5+. For \
0<a<b the central-soft MRK path is\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"75d15828-5155-403c-9b44-63e4c571edaf"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"zeta", "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{"zetaHat", " ", 
       SuperscriptBox["delta", "a"]}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"zetab", "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{"zetaBarHat", " ", 
       SuperscriptBox["delta", "a"]}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      SubsuperscriptBox["Q", "4", "2"], "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{"Q4hat2", " ", 
       SuperscriptBox["delta", 
        RowBox[{"2", " ", "a"}]]}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"X34", "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{"X34hat", " ", 
       SuperscriptBox["delta", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "a"}], "-", "b"}]]}]}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"X45", "\[LongEqual]", 
      FractionBox[
       RowBox[{"X45hat", " ", 
        SuperscriptBox["delta", 
         RowBox[{"a", "-", "b"}]]}], 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"zetaBarHat", " ", 
           SuperscriptBox["delta", "a"]}], "+", "1"}], ")"}], " ", 
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"zetaHat", " ", 
           SuperscriptBox["delta", "a"]}], "+", "1"}], ")"}]}]]}]}], "}"}],
   TraditionalForm],
  TraditionalForm,
  Editable->True]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"6d108b57-9578-49b1-a092-\
e3a17d61a093"],

Cell["\<\
All hatted quantities are fixed and nonzero; zetaBarHat is the complex \
conjugate of zetaHat in the physical domain. In particular, zetaHat and \
zetaBarHat need not be equal, so zeta/zetabar remains generic. The \
denominator in X45 preserves the exact on-shell path. The parity-odd \
invariant is epsilon5=s12 Q4^2 (zeta-zetabar)/(zeta zetabar), so its \
normalized value tends to zero as delta^a.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f97a0613-84ca-478e-a436-eb46fcc4851a"],

Cell["\<\
This limit starts directly from generic five-point kinematics. It does not \
first impose the coplanar condition zeta=zetabar. Near-planar wide-angle \
scattering instead sends zeta-zetabar to zero at fixed Q4^2; central-soft MRK \
sends Q4^2 to zero at fixed transverse angle while maintaining the rapidity \
hierarchy. The two limiting algebraic structures may be compared, but neither \
limit is a sublimit of the other.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"32ca77b6-4458-4e62-bcfc-0aacb4ba9897"],

Cell["Physical rapidity and softness scaling", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"1ec8a9ad-4cd5-4754-841f-97222a910e27"],

Cell["\<\
The rapidity hierarchy is transparent in the light-cone components. Since \
delta tends to zero and 0<a<b, p3 is forward, p5 is backward and p4 remains \
at finite central rapidity:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e76d878a-36aa-4130-9ec4-04d1dc1a3cf6"],

